<article class="env-lemma" id="0199">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0199">Lemma <span data-tag="0199">14.23.5</span>.</a> Let $\mathcal{A}$ be an abelian category. Let $A$ be an object of $\mathcal{A}$ and let $k$ be an integer. We have $N(K(A, k))_ i = A$ if $i = k$ and $0$ else. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      It is clear that $N(K(A, k))_ i = 0$ when $i < k$ because $K(A, k)_ i = 0$ in that case. It is clear that $N(K(A, k))_ k = A$ since $K(A, k)_{k - 1} = 0$ and $K(A, k)_ k = A$. For $i > k$ we have $N(K(A, k))_ i = 0$ by Lemma <a href="/tag/018T" data-tag="018T">14.21.9</a> and the definition of $K(A, k)$, see Definition <a href="/tag/0191" data-tag="0191">14.22.3</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>